# Memory Protocol

Memory is active, not an archive. It is area-tagged and queryable, so relevance is
targeted: query, then read only the hits. Never bulk-read `vault/**`.

## Before the work (mandatory read-gate)

For any non-trivial change (3+ files, financial logic, tenant isolation, contract,
DB migration, sync, installer/release) run the area query first:

```bash
npm run memory:query -- "<task keywords>" --area <area> --limit 8
npm run memory:areas
```

Areas: `ertektar penztar napzaras arfolyam cimletezes sync aml tenant riport
database installer deploy security frontend legacy specifikacio`.

## Mandatory legacy lookup

The memory holds the full analysis of the original Delphi program (gap matrices
over ~645k source lines, Firebird schema reconstruction, DLL parity, RE
reports), the EXCMD requirement corpus (~495 docs) and the recovered survey
knowledge base. The feature being asked for is very often already solved there.

Before any new feature, behaviour change or regression hunt:

```bash
npm run memory:query -- "<feature>" --area legacy --limit 8
npm run memory:query -- "<feature>" --area specifikacio --limit 8
```

State how the legacy solved it and justify every intentional deviation, or say
explicitly that the queries returned nothing.

- Cite the hits (path + the fact used) in the implementation plan, or state
  explicitly that the query returned nothing relevant.
- Debugging and regressions always require the `--area` query: most recurring
  defects already have a recorded root cause.

## Evidence order

- Current repo code, migrations, tests and git state outrank stored memory.
- When memory contradicts the repo, fix the stale memory entry — do not silently
  work around it.

## After the work (mandatory write)

```bash
npm run memory:build
npm run memory:stale-check   # exit 1 = bundle stale, rebuild before closing
```

Save only durable facts: verified root cause, reusable command, project
convention, corrected stale knowledge. Never store secrets or unverified guesses.
